\name{free_bezier}
\alias{free_bezier}
\alias{free_bezier_ring}
\title{
Draw cubic Bezier curves and ribbons
}
\description{
\strong{\code{free_bezier()}:} Draw one cubic Bezier curve on the canvas.

\strong{\code{free_bezier_ring()}:} Draw a filled ribbon bounded by two cubic Bezier curves.
}
\usage{
free_bezier(points, mode = "relative", line_color = "black", line_thickness = 1,
    line_type = "solid")
free_bezier_ring(points1, points2, mode = "relative", fill_color = "grey",
    line_color = NA, line_thickness = 1, line_type = "solid")
}
\arguments{
\item{points}{\strong{\code{free_bezier} only:} Required finite numeric matrix with exactly four rows and two columns (x, y). Rows are start point, first control point, second control point, and end point, in the selected mode.}
\item{mode}{One string: \code{"relative"} (default; canvas width and height are 1), \code{"percent"} (width and height are 100), or \code{"pixel"} (logical pixels). Only positions and geometric widths/heights use this unit; font sizes, gaps and stroke widths remain logical pixels.}
\item{line_color}{\strong{\code{free_bezier}:} Stroke color. Default \code{"black"}; \code{NA} disables the stroke. An R color name, \code{#RRGGBB} or \code{#RRGGBBAA}; alpha is supported.

\strong{\code{free_bezier_ring}:} Boundary stroke color, default \code{NA} (no outline). Supply a color to draw the entire closed boundary. An R color name, \code{#RRGGBB} or \code{#RRGGBBAA}; alpha is supported.}
\item{line_thickness}{One finite nonnegative stroke width in logical pixels. Default 1; 0 hides the stroke.}
\item{line_type}{One string: \code{"solid"} (default), \code{"dashed"}, or \code{"dotted"}.}
\item{points1}{\strong{\code{free_bezier_ring} only:} Required finite numeric four-row, two-column (x, y) matrix: start, control 1, control 2 and end of the first boundary.}
\item{points2}{\strong{\code{free_bezier_ring} only:} Required matrix with the same format for the second boundary, ordered in the same start-to-end direction as points1 (do not reverse its rows).}
\item{fill_color}{\strong{\code{free_bezier_ring} only:} Ribbon fill color, default \code{"grey"}; \code{NA} disables filling. An R color name, \code{#RRGGBB} or \code{#RRGGBBAA}; alpha is supported.}
}
\details{
\subsection{free_bezier}{
Only the start and end points are necessarily on the curve. The middle two points control tangent direction and curvature. The curve is open and has no fill parameter. Coordinates refer to the entire logical canvas, including margins, not to tree or branch coordinates. The origin is the upper-left corner; x increases rightwards and y downwards. Values outside the canvas are not clamped.
}
\subsection{free_bezier_ring}{
The first boundary is traversed forwards, connected to the end of the second boundary, then the second boundary is traversed backwards and connected to the first start point. The result is a closed ribbon, not a circular ring primitive. Coordinates refer to the entire logical canvas, including margins, not to tree or branch coordinates. The origin is the upper-left corner; x increases rightwards and y downwards. Values outside the canvas are not clamped.
}
}
\section{Similarities and differences}{
free_bezier draws one open stroked curve through four control points. free_bezier_ring closes the region between two such boundaries, each ordered in the same direction. The ribbon defaults to grey fill without an outline; the open curve defaults to a black stroke without a fill parameter.
}
\value{
\describe{
\item{\code{free_bezier()}}{A \code{yytree_layer} value; add it to a plot with \code{+}.}
\item{\code{free_bezier_ring()}}{A \code{yytree_layer} value; add it to a plot with \code{+}.}
}
}
